                "correctIndex": 0,
                "passMessage": "Correct! There is always one more term than the power.",
                "failMessage": "For power $n$, the expansion always contains $n+1$ terms."
            },
            "learn": {
                "paragraphs": [
                    "**1. The Theorem:** $(a+b)^n = ^nC_0 a^n + ^nC_1 a^{n-1}b + ^nC_2 a^{n-2}b^2 + \\dots + ^nC_n b^n$.",
                    "**2. Key Observations:**",
                    "- Sum of indices of $a$ and $b$ is always $n$.",
                    "- Coefficients are symmetric ($^nC_r = ^nC_{n-r}$).",
                    "- Number of terms is $n+1$."
                ],
                "formulas": [
                    {
                        "rule": "Sum of Coefficients",
                        "formula": "\\text{Set } a=1, b=1 \\implies 2^n",
                        "example": "\\text{For } (1+x)^3, \\text{ sum is } 1+3+3+1 = 8 = 2^3"
                    }
                ],
                "boxes": [
                    {
                        "type": "tip",
                        "html": "In $(a-b)^n$, the terms alternate signs starting with positive."
                    }
                ]
